{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-87876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $-4-{e}^{3x}>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4-{e}^{3x} & >2 \\\\ -{e}^{3x} & >6 \\\\ {e}^{3x} & <-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $e>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${e}^{3x}$ cannot be less than $-6.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}<-6\n$$\n\nhas no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd0c208406cd7b1934c485494d8158409a602d25fbc07dc42c7103ef1a893fe9"} {"question" "q-87876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x<\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "x>\\frac{2}{3}"] ["text" "all real numbers"] ["text" "no solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "no solutions"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}+27>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}+27 & >0 \\\\ 3\\cdot {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x} & >-27 \\\\ {(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x} & >-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $\\frac{15}{4}>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}$ is greater than $-9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{15}{4})}^{1-x}>-9\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd0c208406cd7b1934c485494d8158409a602d25fbc07dc42c7103ef1a893fe9"} {"question" "q-87891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<1"] ["math" "x<1-{log}_{15/4}⁡(9)"] ["math" "x>1"] ["text" "all real numbers"] ["text" "no solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "all real numbers"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $7\\cdot {4}^{1-x}<{3}^{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot {4}^{1-x} & <{3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(7\\cdot {4}^{1-x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡7+(1-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd0c208406cd7b1934c485494d8158409a602d25fbc07dc42c7103ef1a893fe9"} {"question" "q-87895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}"] ["math" "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}"] ["math" "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}"] ["math" "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}"] ["math" "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-87895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4}{(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 1 \\\\ {(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.62)^{x+5}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62 & \\le 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & \\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd0c208406cd7b1934c485494d8158409a602d25fbc07dc42c7103ef1a893fe9"} {"question" "q-115213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}-5"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}+5"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}{5}-2"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}{10}-2"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}+10"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ (x+1)(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le -\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\ge \\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dd0c208406cd7b1934c485494d8158409a602d25fbc07dc42c7103ef1a893fe9"} {"question" "q-115218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038639/state.json" "file_sha256" "de1926ea3ef12f323e947f664b24be413cbcc3ecff72c259ed04ecab936d6b72"} {"question" "q-115218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1"]] "